== Children Obeying Parents’ Instruction and Correction

=== §. 27. #emph[Of children’s active obedience.]

The affirmative and active part of a child’s obedience consists in making himself pliable to his parents’ will. This must be added to the negative and passive part of obedience named before, of refraining from doing things without the parents’ consent, to show a true childlike affection and disposition toward the parent. Passive obedience may arise from mere sullenness and stubborn pride. For there are many who will refrain from doing this or that without their parents’ consent because they are unwilling to ask for their consent: they would rather have their own wills crossed in the things they desire than be made subject to their parents’ will. What does this show but a stubborn pride and a disdainful heart? Besides, to refrain from doing an unlawful thing is only to #emph[abstain from evil.] But it is required of Christians #footnote[Ps 34:14.] to do what is good, as well as to abstain from what is evil. This is what is commended in Jacob: he not only refrained from taking such a wife as would be a grief to his parents (in which his #footnote[Gen 26:34, 35.] brother Esau had offended), but also obeyed his parents in #footnote[Gen 28:2; 29:18.] taking such a wife as they wished him to take.

We will illustrate this general point by four particular instances, namely, by a child’s obedience to his parents’ #emph[commandments, instructions, reproofs, corrections.]

=== §. 28. #emph[Of children’s obedience to their parents’ commandments.]

I. Whatever lawful #emph[commandments] parents give to their children, the children must be ready to obey them to the utmost of their power. “Obey your#super[+] parents,”#footnote[Eph 6:1.] says the apostle to children.

Parents, by virtue of their place, have power and authority to command. Children therefore must obey, or else that power is to no purpose.

To show this by some particulars:

1\. If a parent calls his child, or sends for him, the child must readily #footnote[A child must come at his parent’s call.] come, indeed even though he does not know the reason. Eli was in the place of a parent to Samuel; so the child, supposing that Eli called him, #footnote[1 Sam 3:5, #emph[etc.]] ran to him once, and again, and again. David,#footnote[1 Sam 16:12.] when he was sent for by his father out of the field to be anointed king, did not know the reason, yet he came. #footnote[Gen 49:1.] The twelve sons of Jacob, though grown men, yet when their father called for them, assembled themselves together before him.

2\. If a parent is minded to send his child anywhere, or on #footnote[A child must go at his parent’s word.] any errand, though it is far off and may seem somewhat troublesome, yet the child ought to go and do it. The #footnote[§. 27.] example of Jacob named before,#footnote[Gen 28:5.] and also the examples of Joseph#footnote[Gen 37:14.] (sent to see whether all was well with his brothers), of the ten sons of Jacob#footnote[Gen 42:2, 3.] (sent by their father into Egypt), and of David#footnote[1 Sam 17:17.] (sent to visit his brothers in the camp), are commended by the Holy Spirit in this case. Of David it is noted that #footnote[1 Sam 17:20.] he “got up early … and set out as Jesse had commanded him,” which shows his ready obedience.

3\. If a parent requires his child to wait on him, the child must #footnote[A child at command must wait on the parent.] do that too. When Abraham was going up to the top of Moriah, his will was that his servants should stay behind, and that his son Isaac should go with him and carry the wood for the sacrifice; and Isaac obeyed accordingly. #footnote[Gen 22:6.]

4\. If a parent gives any task, or commits any business, to #footnote[A child must do the errand his parent would have him do.] his child, the child ought to perform it faithfully. This kind of faithful obedience is commended in Joseph,#footnote[Gen 50:5.] in the Rechabites,#footnote[Jer 35:8.] and in David,#footnote[1 Sam 17:20.] with many others. Joseph, because of his high position, was not free to go out of Egypt; yet, to perform what his father had charged him with, he asked leave. The Rechabites were tempted to break their father’s charge, yet they would not. David, when he was sent by his father from the sheep which had been committed to his keeping, took care to leave them “with a keeper”;#footnote[1 Sam 17:20.] and again, when a bear at one time and a lion at another came #footnote[#emph[v.] 34.] to the flock, he put his life in danger to preserve the flock. All these circumstances are noted in this way to set out the great care these children had to discharge the charge which their parents had committed to them.

=== §. 29. #emph[Of children’s disobedience to their parents’ commandments.]

Contrary to this is a rebellious disposition in children, shown by these and similar practices:

1\. By refusing to come at their parents’ call; or by coming (as we say) at their leisure, and making their parents wait for them.

2\. By lazily and sluggishly offering vain and frivolous excuses when their parents would send them on an errand, like that sluggard who says, “There is a lion outside! I will be slain in the streets!”#footnote[Prov 22:13.] Such pretexts are “like vinegar to the teeth and smoke to the eyes.”#footnote[Prov 10:26.]

3\. By scorning to wait on their parents. For this reason they will slip out of doors and absent themselves when they imagine their parents will need their service in that way: they, if you please, will not be their parents’ servants. An ungodly notion!

4\. By refusing to do what their parents enjoin on them and require of them, like #footnote[Matt 21:30.] the younger son who did not go to work in the vineyard at his father’s command. For the most part, those children offend in this who through pride think the business given them too lowly and base to do. Had David#footnote[1 Sam 16:13, 21; 17:15.] or the daughters of Reuel#footnote[Exod 2:16, 17.] been of this mind, the one would not have returned to his father’s sheep after he was anointed to be king over Israel and after he had been called to the court; and the others would not have watered their father’s sheep, especially among such rude and rowdy louts as would drive them away without any respect for their place and sex.

=== §. 30. #emph[Of children’s obedience to their parents’ instruction.]

#smallcaps[II]. Such wholesome #emph[instructions] as parents give their children for the right ordering of their conduct, children ought conscientiously to obey. Solomon#footnote[Prov 1:8, 9; 4:1.] presses this point much. “Listen, my son,” he says, “to your father’s instruction, and do not forsake the teaching of your mother,” etc. And to move children all the more to do so, he sets before them #footnote[Prov 4:3.] his own example, showing that he required no more of them than he himself had performed. Moses,#footnote[Exod 18:24.] though grown to years and a prince among his people, showed his obedience to his father-in-law in this.

1\. Parents have an express commandment to instruct their #footnote[Reasons. 1. Parents ought to instruct.] children (as we will show later).#footnote[Treatise 6, §. 26, #emph[etc.]] There is great reason, therefore, that their children should listen to them and obey them in this. The good instructions of anyone are to be regarded; much more those of parents.

2\. Great wisdom may be gained by it. For the desire parents have for their children’s good makes them give the best #footnote[2\. Children get much wisdom by it.] directions they can to them, namely what they themselves have learned from others or observed by their own experience. In this respect Solomon calls him “a wise son” who “heeds his father’s instruction,”#footnote[Prov 13:1.] and he likens its fruit and benefit to a graceful ornament, to chains and bracelets, and to a crown of glory.#footnote[Prov 1:9; 4:9.]

3\. Much joy and comfort is brought to parents by seeing #footnote[3\. Much joy is brought to parents.] their children observe their instruction; for “a wise son brings joy to his father.”#footnote[Prov 10:1.] Now this is something children ought to aim at: to bring joy to their parents’ hearts.#footnote[Prov 27:11.] Esau is blamed for #emph[grieving]#footnote[Gen 26:35.] his parents.

Contrary to this is the proud and foolish attitude of those who think they need no instruction: their parents are too suspicious of them, they are wise enough by themselves, and if their parents would only leave them alone, they would do better. In this way they show themselves ungodly toward God, rebellious against their parents, and harmful to themselves. Such were Eli’s#footnote[1 Sam 2:25.] and Lot’s#footnote[Gen 19:14.] sons. Now note the vengeance that fell on them.

=== §. 31. #emph[Of children’s patience under their parents’ reproof.]

#smallcaps[III]. The obedience of children must further #footnote[Treatise 3, §. 47, etc.] #footnote[The reasons given and the directions laid down concerning a wife’s subjection to her husband’s reproof may fitly be applied to this part of children’s obedience. Here therefore the same order is observed, and the separate points are confirmed by proofs pertinent to children.] extend to their parents’ reproof: both by #emph[patiently bearing] every kind of reproof, and by #emph[amending] what is justly reproved. That #emph[shame] which the Lord speaks of, which should be in a child #footnote[Num 12:14.] when “her father had but spit in her face” (that is, had shown his anger by some outward sign), implies a child’s patient bearing of a parent’s reproof. This patience in a child must be shown to his parent whether the reproof is mild or bitter, just or unjust. In this respect a child must consider the #emph[person] who reproves more than the #emph[matter] or #emph[manner] of the reproof. Jacob’s reproof of Joseph for his dreams, which came from God, was unjust;#footnote[Gen 37:10.] and the manner of uttering it, with many short pauses and in questions, implies some tartness. Yet Joseph’s patience was such that we do not read of one discontented word that he gave. But most memorable is the patience of Jonathan in this respect. His father Saul’s#footnote[1 Sam 20:30, #emph[etc.]] reproof of him was plainly unjust and bitter beyond measure; yet with what patience he bore it! All he replied was to make some small defense of David; he replied nothing against his father’s insulting words.

Great wisdom may be learned by this patience. For in this way a #footnote[#emph[Reason.] Wisdom learned.] child can better judge whether his parents’ reproof is just or not. Though it is unjust, yet by it he may observe what displeases his parents; at least he may observe his parents’ weakness, and so know better how to conduct himself toward them.

Contrary to this is the practice of those children who, at every reproof of their parents, are ready to #emph[answer back.] “Our parents #footnote[See §. 6.] are so wayward,” they say; “who can bear them?” If no one else could bear them, yet children should. For parents ordinarily bear such waywardness and unruliness from their children, especially while they are young, as no one else would or could.

#emph[Question.] If a parent is mistaken in a matter and unjustly reproves his child, may the child make no answer?

#emph[Answer.] Yes, he may, provided he does it mildly, reverently and at a fitting time, not too flatly crossing or contradicting his parent.

#emph[Objection.] Christ rebuked his mother very bluntly for reproving #footnote[Luke 2:49.] him unjustly.

#emph[Answer.] Christ, as God-man, was greater than his mother, and in that respect he blamed her with authority for her unjust reproof. The Virgin Mary was not ignorant of this, and therefore she was silent.

=== §. 32. #emph[Of children’s readiness to amend what is justly reproved by their parents.]

If a child is justly reproved by his parents for his fault, #footnote[See Treatise 3, §. 48.] both conscience toward God and obedience to his parent require that he readily put right what is amiss. Unless amendment of the thing justly reproved is added to patient bearing of reproof, that patience can be counted no better than pretense and plain mockery. When the father-in-law #footnote[Exod 18:17.] of Moses told him that what he was doing was “not good,” he amended it at once.

But on the contrary, many wicked and graceless children go on in their evil courses, though their parents rebuke them for it again and again. Eli’s reproof of his sons was just, yet no amendment followed. Now note the inference the Holy Spirit makes from this: “They would not listen #footnote[1 Sam 2:25.] to their father, since the #smallcaps[Lord] intended to put them to death.” By this it is implied that to despise the just reproof of parents is a clear sign and forerunner of God’s heavy judgment. Solomon calls the child who “does not listen to rebuke” “a mocker,”#footnote[Prov 13:1.] which points to a most obstinate sinner who cannot be reclaimed, and who for that reason is #emph[mocked] by the Lord.#footnote[Prov 3:34.]

=== §. 33. #emph[Of children’s submission to their parents’ correction.]

#smallcaps[IIII]. Correction is a reproof in deed, a reproof in the highest degree, indeed the severest kind of reproof; so that submitting to it is a great trial of obedience. A child’s submission to his parent in this kind of reproof must be shown by the same means as in the former, namely:

1\. By patiently bearing the correction his parent gives him.

2\. By readily amending what he is justly corrected for.

The apostle notes the first of these as #emph[a settled case,] a matter not to be denied, in these words: “We have had fathers of our flesh who corrected us, and we gave them reverence.”#footnote[Heb 12:9.] One special part of this #emph[reverence] is #emph[patient endurance;] therefore he infers from it, “Should we not much more submit,” etc.

Solomon sets out the second by an effect that follows on performing it. For having advised a parent to correct his child, he adds this reason: “He will give you rest; he will give delight to your soul.”#footnote[Prov 29:17.] How can this #emph[rest] and #emph[delight] be given except by the child’s amending what he is corrected for? A parent takes no delight in the pain and smart of his child, but in the fruit that follows from it. As a child’s transgression is #emph[a grief]#footnote[Gen 26:35.] and vexation to the parent, so his amendment brings #emph[rest] and #emph[delight.] Now this effect does not follow from correction in itself, but from the good use the child makes of it. It lies therefore in the child, and so lies on him as a duty, to give this #emph[rest] and #emph[delight] to his parent by amending the fault for which he is corrected, as he brought grief to him by provoking him to use correction. In this way neither will the parent regret #emph[inflicting] correction, nor the child regret #emph[enduring] it.

So that a child may attain this degree of obedience, he #footnote[Direction for using correction well.] must duly consider both the #emph[cause] that moves his parent to correct him and the #emph[end] his parent aims at in it. The #emph[cause] is the #emph[love]#footnote[Prov 13:24.] he bears his child. The #emph[end] he aims at is his #emph[child’s good.]#footnote[Prov 22:15.] If these motives do not produce obedience, what can?

=== §. 34. #emph[Of refusing, or abusing correction.]

Contrary to this is #emph[disdain] on the one side and #emph[obstinacy] on the other. #emph[Disdain,] when children scorn to be corrected by their parents; and so, when they cannot avoid it by any means they can use, they will mutter and murmur, fret and fume, rage and rave against their parents, and despise and hate them for it. #emph[Obstinacy,] when they will not be bettered by it in the least, but keep running on in their wicked courses, and rather grow worse for being corrected. This may be counted the highest pitch of a child’s rebellion, for this is the last means a parent can use to reclaim his child from desperate courses. If this does not prevail, the law of God requires #footnote[Deut 21:18. #emph[etc.]] that a parent give up his child into the hand of the magistrate, so that he may be put to death.

#emph[So much for the separate branches of children’s obedience.]

#emph[Its extent follows.]

=== §. 35. #emph[Of children’s conforming their judgments to their parents.]

The #emph[extent] of children’s obedience is only implied in this epistle to the Ephesians, but it is expressed (Col 3:20) in these words: “Children, obey your#super[+] parents #footnote[κατὰ πάντα.] IN ALL THINGS.” It is a large extent, but not to be taken absolutely, without any limitation; for the apostle himself notes a restraint in these words, #footnote[Eph 6:1 ἐν κυρίῳ.] #emph[in the Lord.] So far as children do not transgress any of God’s commandments in obeying their parents, they ought to obey. This is to obey #emph[in all things, in the Lord.]

The#footnote[Treatise 3, §. 63, 64, etc. The extent of children’s obedience.] #emph[extent] of children’s duties is the very same as that of wives’ duties, and the #emph[restraint] also is the same; so the order observed there will be kept here also. Only other proofs, more pertinent to children’s place, will be brought to confirm those general propositions which may be applied to any inferiors. Many general reasons given there in proof of the propositions will be omitted here. Therefore compare this place with that.

So we see that parents’ authority is very large: there is no restraint on it but God’s contrary command, of which a child must be sure if he refuses to obey his parent in anything.

It is not enough for a child to say, “I have obeyed my parent so long and in so many things; I hope that if I have my own will in some things, I may be excused.” No: #emph[all things] covers more than #emph[many things.] So #emph[many] are not enough. And though God’s will is exempted, your own will is not exempted. Though you may do nothing against God’s will, yet you ought to do many things against your own will, if it is contrary to your parents’.

Children must work at two things in order to attain this #emph[extent] of obedience #emph[in all things.]

1\. They must work to bring their judgment and will into line with their parents’: to think fitting and suitable for them to do what their parents want them to do. Though Isaac thought it somewhat strange that he should #footnote[Gen 22:6, 7.] carry wood up a hill to offer a sacrifice where there was nothing for a burnt offering, yet since it was his father’s will that he should do so, he thought it fitting enough for him to do so.

This subjection of judgment and will is to be given in all the particular cases of obedience set out #footnote[§. 13. 14. #emph[etc.]] before, as in their #emph[calling, marriage, clothing, allowance,] etc. So children are to think that kind of calling, that particular match, that clothing and that allowance most fitting for them which their parents think fitting.

If the judgment is persuaded that a thing is fitting, #footnote[#emph[Reason.]] and the will is inwardly brought to yield to it, outward obedience will be given to it more readily and cheerfully.

Contrary to this is the overweening opinion which many children #footnote[Children’s overweening opinion of their own judgment above their parents’ causes much harm.] have of their own judgment and will: they think they can discern better than their parents what is fit and proper for themselves. They imagine their parents to be too strict and precise, or too suspicious and distrustful, or too covetous and worldly. This makes them take whatever callings, matches, clothing and allowance they think best; from which many harms arise, which would all easily be avoided if they would lay down that presumptuous opinion and labor to follow the direction given above.

=== §. 36. #emph[Of children’s yielding to do at their parents’ command such things as in their judgment they cannot think very fitting.]

2\. Though children cannot think in their judgment that what their parents require is the fittest and most suitable, yet, being pressed to it by their parents’ peremptory command, they ought to yield to it in practice, saying to their parents, as Peter said to the Lord, “But because you say so, I will do this.” Jacob#footnote[Gen 27:6. #emph[etc.]] yielded to Rebekah in this way. He thought that by doing what his mother told him he would seem to be mocking his father; yet when she urged him, he did it.

#emph[Question.] If a child can give a better reason than his parent, is he not allowed to refuse to do what he thinks unfitting, or at least to hold back from doing what he is commanded until he is better informed that it is fitting?

#emph[Answer.] With reverence and humility he may give his reason why he thinks it unfitting, and ask his parent not to press it on him.

(Judah,#footnote[Gen 43:3.] one of the sons of Jacob, did this, and is not blamed for it), and parents ought in such a case to yield to their children (as Jacob#footnote[#emph[v.] 11.] did). But yet if, in things neither commanded nor forbidden, the parents are otherwise minded than their children, and want their children to yield to them, the children must yield.

For, 1. In things neither commanded nor forbidden, a parent’s command is a warrant to the child, because of this extent #emph[(all things);] so that the parent may sin in commanding a thing which the child may do without sin. Who can clear Rebekah of sin in commanding Jacob to deceive his father? Yet I take it that Jacob cannot justly be blamed for obeying.

2\. In this way children show a high esteem for their parents and very great respect toward them; they show how eager they are to please them and how afraid to offend them. When the will of parent and child agree, there is no such test.

3\. By this means peace and love are better preserved between parent and child: a parent’s anger is stopped, its effects avoided, and many other harms prevented, which often happen when inferiors refuse to yield to their superiors who have authority over them.

Contrary to this is the wrongheaded peremptoriness of those who will do #footnote[A fault for children to be too peremptory against their parents’ command.] nothing against their own mind and will, however much their parents require it. This phrase#footnote[Gen 43:5.] (“If you will not send him, we will not go down”), which Judah used to his father, though in a good cause, was too peremptory for a child. Those who obstinately refuse to do the things that are against their own mind must come short of this extent, #emph[obey in all things.] Indeed, they show that what they do is done for their own sakes, because they like it, rather than for their parents’ sake. What sort of obedience can that be thought to be? Yet this is all the obedience many children will give. If they do not think what their parents require is fitting, neither fair nor foul means will move them to do it; and by this many children greatly provoke their parents. Let such children know that it is in every way safer for them, at the pressing command of their parent, to do what they think unfitting than peremptorily to disobey their parents, which is worse than unfitting: it is unlawful.

=== §. 37. #emph[Of the restraint of children’s obedience.]

The #emph[restraint] of children’s obedience is expressed in this clause, #emph[in the Lord.] This phrase gives a necessary limitation #footnote[Heb 12:9 τῆς σαρκός.] to their obeying their parents, who are only #emph[parents of our flesh,] men and women, liable to err in their commandments and to require such sinful things as their children cannot perform with a good conscience. The limitation, then, which this clause #emph[(in the Lord)] gives is this:

#emph[Children must give no other obedience to their parents than may stand with their obedience to God.] The reasons the apostle gives prove as much: “This is right”; “this is pleasing to #footnote[Eph 6:1. Col 3:20.] the Lord.” But to obey parents against the Lord is neither right nor pleasing to the Lord.

If, therefore, parents command their children to do anything #footnote[See the two pairs of cautions attached to the like limitations of a wife’s obedience, Treatise 3, §. 51. & 52. and apply them to these limitations of children’s obedience.] which the Lord has forbidden them, they ought not to do it. On this ground Michal did well in letting her husband David escape out of the hands of Saul her father.#footnote[1 Sam 19:11. #emph[etc.]] I do not justify her way of carrying out the matter, with untruths and false tales; but her refusal to yield to her father’s mind and will is justifiable, and that in two respects.

1\. Because the dispute was between her husband and her father. Now #footnote[Gen 2:24; 3:16.] by God’s law a wife is to yield to her husband rather than to her father.

2\. Because she knew her father sought to kill him. If she had then delivered him into her father’s hands, she would have made herself an accessory to murder. In this latter respect Jonathan also did well in refusing to fetch David at his father’s #footnote[1 Sam 20:31, 32.] command.

So if a father commands his child to go to Mass, to perjure himself, to marry an idolater, to steal, to lie, or to commit any other sin forbidden by God, the child ought not to obey: those things cannot be done #emph[in the Lord.]

Again, if parents forbid their children to do any necessary duty commanded by God, the child ought to do it despite the parents’ prohibition. We may well think that Ahaz, who set himself so violently to deface the holy things of God, to profane his ordinances, and to shut up the doors of God’s house, gave strict orders to his son that he #footnote[2 Chr 29:3. #emph[etc.]] should not repair them again; yet Hezekiah, as soon as he had power, repaired them all.

If a parent forbids his child to go to the Protestant churches, to hear a sermon, to pray in a language he understands, to give just weight and measure, to speak the truth when he is called to witness to it, and the like, the child must be of Daniel’s mind, #footnote[Dan 6:10.] and despite that prohibition do the things God requires.

=== §. 38. #emph[Of children’s sin in yielding to their parents against God.]

Contrary to this limitation is, on the one side, a flattering eye-service in many children, who do not care what they do, whether #footnote[1\. Eye-service.] it is good or evil, lawful or unlawful, so long as they may please their parents by it; and on the other side, a slavish fearfulness, #footnote[2\. Fearfulness.] which makes them dread their parents so much that they do not fear God at all. They would rather choose to sin, and so provoke God’s wrath, than do anything by which their parents’ wrath may be provoked. It is a brand set on evil kings that they #footnote[1 Kgs 22:52.] walked in the ways of their fathers and mothers, and did wickedly #footnote[2 Chr 22:4.] as they counseled them. So their following and obeying their parents in evil was so far from lessening their sin that it rather made it worse. The preferring of father and mother before the Lord Christ shows that such a child is “not worthy” of Christ.#footnote[Matt 10:37.] In comparison with Christ, father and mother must be hated.#footnote[Luke 14:26.] But it is that undue and unchristian regard for parents above Christ that makes so many young papists, young swaggerers, swearers, #footnote[Motives to stir up children to prefer God before their parents.] liars, deceitful persons and people of wicked life.

To avoid the two extremes just named, let your heart be filled with a true fear of God, and also consider the difference between our earthly parents and our heavenly Father. They are only parents “of our flesh”;#footnote[Heb 12:9.] he is “the Father of our spirits.” #footnote[Luke 12:4, 5.] They can only touch the body; he can cast body and soul into hell. They are over us only for a while; he forever. Their authority is subordinate to his; his is supreme and absolute in itself. They can give only a light, temporary reward; he, an eternal weight of glory. They cannot shelter us from his wrath; he can shelter us from theirs.

So much for those duties of children which concern their parents’ authority; those which concern their necessity follow.
